; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Dynaturtle
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     Mindstorms, physics, keyboard, velocity, WRAP
; SUMMARY:  Papert's dynaturtle: a turtle that keeps moving after you kick it, so you have to think like Newton to park it on a target.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   This turtle does not stop when you stop pressing keys. A kick
;   changes its speed, and it keeps drifting in a straight line until
;   another kick changes that. The game is to stop the turtle on the
;   yellow target. Turning does NOT change the way it drifts, which is
;   the surprising part.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run, then CLICK THE TURTLE WORLD so it gets your keys.
;     LEFT / RIGHT arrows   turn the turtle 15 degrees
;     UP arrow or space     kick it forward (in the way it is facing)
;     Q                     quit
;   To stop, turn the turtle to face backwards and kick. Park on the
;   target with a speed under 0.6 to score. Press Stop to end.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * velocity as two numbers, a sideways speed and an up-down speed
;   * a kick adds to the velocity using SIN and COS of the heading
;   * WRAP mode, so the turtle leaves one side and comes back on the other
;   * a game loop that reads keys with KEYP and READCHAR without waiting
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Add a little friction: multiply both speeds by 0.99 every step.
;   * Make kicks stronger or weaker.
;   * Add gravity: take 0.02 off the up-down speed every step.
; ============================================================

; In Mindstorms (1980) Seymour Papert describes the "dynaturtle", a
; turtle that obeys Newton's laws of motion. Children (and, he notes,
; physics students) expected it to go where it was pointing, and were
; surprised that it kept its old motion. Playing with it builds a feel
; for velocity that a page of equations does not.

to setup
  clearscreen
  wrap
  setbackground :sky
  setshape "arrow
  setpencolor [120 200 255]
  penup home
  showturtle
  make "vx 0
  make "vy 0
  make "score 0
  make "target [0 0]
  new.target
  print [Click the turtle world. Arrows turn, UP or space kicks, Q quits.]
end

; Erase the old target (paint over it in the background color) and put
; a new one somewhere random.
to new.target
  draw.target :target "true
  make "target list (random 600) - 300 (random 400) - 200
  draw.target :target "false
end

; The target is a yellow ring: a big yellow dot with a smaller dot of
; the background color in the middle.
to draw.target :place :erase
  localmake "color pencolor
  setpensize 36
  ifelse :erase [setpencolor :sky] [setpencolor [250 210 50]]
  dot :place
  setpensize 18
  setpencolor :sky
  dot :place
  setpensize 1
  setpencolor :color
end

; A kick of strength 0.5 in the direction the turtle faces. Heading 0
; is up, so the sideways part uses SIN and the up-down part uses COS.
to kick
  make "vx :vx + 0.5 * sin heading
  make "vy :vy + 0.5 * cos heading
  tone 180 15
end

to keys
  if not keyp [stop]
  localmake "k readchar
  if emptyp :k [stop]
  if equalp :k "LEFT [left 15]
  if equalp :k "RIGHT [right 15]
  if or equalp :k "UP equalp :k "| | [kick]
  if equalp :k "q [throw "quit]
end

to speed
  output sqrt :vx * :vx + :vy * :vy
end

to check.target
  if (distance :target) > 14 [stop]
  if speed > 0.6 [stop]
  make "score :score + 1
  print (sentence [Parked! Score:] :score)
  sound [523 80 659 80 784 120]
  new.target
end

; One tick: read a key, drift, check. SETXY in WRAP mode wraps the turtle
; round the edges for us, so we just add the velocity to the position.
to tick
  keys
  setxy xcor + :vx ycor + :vy
  check.target
  wait 1
end

make "sky [15 20 40]
setup
catch "quit [forever [tick]]
print (sentence [Bye! You parked] :score [times.])
